[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The assets worth attacking, who would attack them, how, the goals that answer
each threat, and the risk that remains.

These were five markdown tables in [`security.md`](security.md), promoted here
for the reason the architecture decisions and the hazards went to
[`04_software_arch.sdoc`](04_software_arch.sdoc) and [`safety.sdoc`](safety.sdoc): they
are the part of the concept that has identity. Forty-eight stable IDs with a
chain between them that was previously kept by hand in an appendix.

The chain is now relations the build checks:

- `Threatens` from each threat to the asset it is aimed at, and `Exploited by`
  to the adversary who can mount it,
- `Reaches` from a threat to the **safety hazard** it arrives at, which is the
  claim this document exists to make and the one that was hardest to see:
  [`HZ-12`](safety.sdoc) is a node now, so "a security compromise of this device
  is a safety event" is a link rather than a sentence,
- `Addresses` from each security goal to the threats it answers, and
  `Realised by` to the requirements behind it,
- `Remains under` from each residual risk to the goal that leaves it.

Two fields carry what the markdown carried as typography, and both are worth
more as fields.

**STATUS** replaces strikethrough. Four threats, two goals and one residual risk
were closed or dissolved by the read-only decision of 2026-10-06, and in the
markdown that was a `~~struck-through heading~~` plus a sentence. It is now
`Resolved` or `Withdrawn`, which can be filtered, counted and reported on. A
closed threat is kept rather than deleted: the reasoning for why it is closed is
the most useful thing about it, and a reader who finds the ID in an old commit
should land on an explanation.

**IMPLEMENTATION** replaces the `[built]` / `[partial]` / `[spec]` / `[absent]`
marks on the goals. `security.md` says the preponderance of `[spec]` is the
single most important fact in it; a typed field makes that countable instead of
a thing a reader has to notice.

Everything else stays in [`security.md`](security.md): the scope, the trust
boundary diagram, section 7 on security as a safety concern, what the
implementation already gets right, the obligations, the verification status and
the open questions. The trust boundaries `B-1` to `B-6` stay there too, and that
is a decision rather than an omission: they are the legend of the diagram
immediately above them, they carry no chain through them, and splitting a legend
from its picture helps nobody.

A reconciliation, stated because it changed what some relations say. The
appendix tables at the end of `security.md` were not updated by the 2026-10-06
read-only change, while sections 5, 6 and 9 were, so the two disagreed. Where
they disagree the body is taken as authoritative. The consequence is visible
and deliberate: with `SEC-01` and `SEC-04` withdrawn, the threats the stale
appendix routed through them ([LINK: TH-08] and [LINK: TH-11]) now have no
security goal at all, and so does [LINK: TH-13], which never had one. That is a
real gap, and naming it is better than inheriting a mapping to a goal that no
longer exists.
<<<

[[SECTION]]
UID: SECURITY-1
TITLE: 1. Assets

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
What is worth attacking, in the order an attacker would care about.
<<<

[ASSET]
UID: A-1
STATEMENT: >>>
**Control of the heater.**
<<<
WHY_IT_MATTERS: >>>
The kiln is a multi-kilowatt mains heater reaching 1350 °C. This is the asset; everything else is a means to it.
<<<

[ASSET]
UID: A-2
STATEMENT: >>>
**The safety configuration.**
<<<
WHY_IT_MATTERS: >>>
Maximum chamber temperature, runaway thresholds, current limits. Loosening these does not start a fire by itself, but it removes the layer that would stop one.
<<<

[ASSET]
UID: A-3
STATEMENT: >>>
**The latched-fault state.**
<<<
WHY_IT_MATTERS: >>>
A latched fault is the mechanism that keeps a failed kiln off ([LINK: SWR-SAF-17]). Clearing one remotely re-arms a kiln that something has already gone wrong with.
<<<

[ASSET]
UID: A-4
STATEMENT: >>>
**WiFi credentials.**
<<<
WHY_IT_MATTERS: >>>
`net.wifi_pass` is the user's network password, stored on the device. Its loss is a breach of their network, not merely of this device.
<<<

[ASSET]
UID: A-5
STATEMENT: >>>
**The web password.**
<<<
WHY_IT_MATTERS: >>>
`security.web_password`, guarding [LINK: A-1] to [LINK: A-3]. Removed from the configuration schema on 2026-10-06; the asset is kept because the threats against it are part of why it is gone.
<<<

[ASSET]
UID: A-6
STATEMENT: >>>
**The firmware image.**
<<<
WHY_IT_MATTERS: >>>
Write access to it is total and persistent control of [LINK: A-1].
<<<

[ASSET]
UID: A-7
STATEMENT: >>>
**Firing programs and run history.**
<<<
WHY_IT_MATTERS: >>>
Commercially meaningful to a production potter; the lowest-value asset here, and the only one whose loss is purely a privacy matter.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: SECURITY-2
TITLE: 2. Adversaries

[ADVERSARY]
UID: ADV-1
STATEMENT: >>>
**Another device on the LAN**: a compromised IoT gadget, a guest laptop, malware on a household machine.
<<<
CAPABILITY: >>>
Full IP access to the device; no credentials.
<<<
REALISTIC: >>>
**Yes.** This is the normal case and the one [LINK: SYS-ASM-05] quietly assumes away.
<<<

[ADVERSARY]
UID: ADV-2
STATEMENT: >>>
**A malicious web page** the operator visits while on the same LAN.
<<<
CAPABILITY: >>>
Can make cross-origin requests from the operator's browser; can attempt DNS rebinding to defeat the same-origin policy.
<<<
REALISTIC: >>>
**Yes**, and it needs no LAN foothold at all.
<<<

[ADVERSARY]
UID: ADV-3
STATEMENT: >>>
**Someone with brief physical access**: a shared studio, a classroom, a workshop.
<<<
CAPABILITY: >>>
UART, JTAG, flash readout, reflash.
<<<
REALISTIC: >>>
**Yes**, given where kilns live.
<<<

[ADVERSARY]
UID: ADV-4
STATEMENT: >>>
**A later owner** of a second-hand or disposed device.
<<<
CAPABILITY: >>>
Everything in flash.
<<<
REALISTIC: >>>
**Yes**, and nobody thinks about it.
<<<

[ADVERSARY]
UID: ADV-5
STATEMENT: >>>
**An attacker from the internet.**
<<<
CAPABILITY: >>>
Only if the user port-forwards or the router is compromised.
<<<
REALISTIC: >>>
Only through a user error that the documentation must argue against.
<<<

[ADVERSARY]
UID: ADV-6
STATEMENT: >>>
**A supply-chain attacker** on a dependency.
<<<
CAPABILITY: >>>
Code execution in the image.
<<<
REALISTIC: >>>
Low: no package manager at build time, assets vendored ([LINK: UR-CON-04]), dependencies are ESP-IDF itself.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: SECURITY-3
TITLE: 3. Threats

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Rated by consequence, not likelihood. A `Reaches` relation to a hazard means the
threat arrives at [`safety.sdoc`](safety.sdoc)'s analysis and therefore at
everything behind it; `SAFETY_RELEVANT` says so in a field as well, because the
distinction between "yes", "indirectly" and "closed" is the one a reader scans
for.
<<<

[THREAT]
UID: TH-01
STATEMENT: >>>
**Unauthenticated command execution.**
<<<
CONSEQUENCE: >>>
Would have been direct control of the heater.
<<<
SAFETY_RELEVANT: Closed
STATUS: Resolved
COMMENT: >>>
**Closed by [LINK: SWR-WEB-26].** No network route can start a firing, abort one or command manual duty; the handlers are deleted, not disabled.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: A-1
  ROLE: Threatens
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HZ-12
  ROLE: Reaches
- TYPE: Child
  VALUE: SWR-WEB-26
  ROLE: Closed by

[THREAT]
UID: TH-02
STATEMENT: >>>
**Loosening the safety configuration.**
<<<
CONSEQUENCE: >>>
Would have removed the layer that stops a runaway rather than starting one.
<<<
SAFETY_RELEVANT: Closed
STATUS: Resolved
COMMENT: >>>
**Closed by [LINK: SWR-WEB-26].** `/api/config` is read-only; the maximum temperature and every safety threshold are writable only at the kiln. [LINK: SWR-SAF-22] and [LINK: SWR-SAF-23] bounded the damage even before that, which is the point section 7 of [`security.md`](security.md) makes about bounds doing security work.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: A-2
  ROLE: Threatens
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HZ-02
  ROLE: Reaches
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HZ-10
  ROLE: Reaches
- TYPE: Child
  VALUE: SWR-WEB-26
  ROLE: Closed by

[THREAT]
UID: TH-03
STATEMENT: >>>
**Clearing a latched fault remotely.**
<<<
CONSEQUENCE: >>>
Would have re-armed a kiln that something had already gone wrong with.
<<<
SAFETY_RELEVANT: Closed
STATUS: Resolved
COMMENT: >>>
**Closed by [LINK: SWR-WEB-26].** Acknowledgement happens in front of the kiln. The fault stays readable, which is what the banner needs. [LINK: SWR-SAF-18] blunted it regardless: a fault cannot be cleared while its condition is still observably true, so an attacker could clear a stale fault but not a live one.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: A-3
  ROLE: Threatens
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HZ-08
  ROLE: Reaches
- TYPE: Child
  VALUE: SWR-WEB-26
  ROLE: Closed by

[THREAT]
UID: TH-04
STATEMENT: >>>
**Hostile firmware upload.**
<<<
CONSEQUENCE: >>>
Would have been total and persistent control of the heater.
<<<
SAFETY_RELEVANT: Closed
STATUS: Resolved
COMMENT: >>>
**Closed by [LINK: SWR-UPD-01].** No image is accepted over the network. Note the cost: the device now has **no field update path at all** ([LINK: OQ-08]), so a security fix needs a physical visit. Carried as [LINK: SRR-11], which blocks release.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: A-6
  ROLE: Threatens
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HZ-01
  ROLE: Reaches
- TYPE: Child
  VALUE: SWR-UPD-01
  ROLE: Closed by

[THREAT]
UID: TH-05
STATEMENT: >>>
**Cross-site request forgery and DNS rebinding.**
<<<
VIA: >>>
[LINK: ADV-2]; the operator's own browser is the confused deputy.
<<<
CONSEQUENCE: >>>
Reduced by [LINK: TH-01] to [LINK: TH-04] being closed: the worst a forged request can now do is **edit a stored firing program**, which takes effect only if an operator starts it at the kiln.
<<<
SAFETY_RELEVANT: Indirect
STATUS: Active
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: A-7
  ROLE: Threatens
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ADV-2
  ROLE: Exploited by
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HZ-12
  ROLE: Reaches

[THREAT]
UID: TH-06
STATEMENT: >>>
**Credential disclosure from flash.**
<<<
VIA: >>>
[LINK: ADV-3], [LINK: ADV-4], plaintext NVS, no flash encryption.
<<<
CONSEQUENCE: >>>
[LINK: A-4], the user's WiFi password, and [LINK: A-5].
<<<
SAFETY_RELEVANT: No
STATUS: Active
COMMENT: >>>
Not a safety event, but it breaches the user's network rather than only this device.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: A-4
  ROLE: Threatens
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: A-5
  ROLE: Threatens
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ADV-3
  ROLE: Exploited by
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ADV-4
  ROLE: Exploited by

[THREAT]
UID: TH-07
STATEMENT: >>>
**Credential disclosure on the wire.**
<<<
VIA: >>>
No TLS; the password crosses the LAN in cleartext on every authenticated request.
<<<
CONSEQUENCE: >>>
[LINK: A-5], observable by [LINK: ADV-1].
<<<
SAFETY_RELEVANT: No
STATUS: Active
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: A-5
  ROLE: Threatens
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ADV-1
  ROLE: Exploited by

[THREAT]
UID: TH-08
STATEMENT: >>>
**Password brute force.**
<<<
VIA: >>>
Repeated requests against the auth check.
<<<
CONSEQUENCE: >>>
[LINK: A-5].
<<<
SAFETY_RELEVANT: No
STATUS: Active
COMMENT: >>>
**Has no security goal.** It was answered by `SEC-04`, which was withdrawn on 2026-10-06 along with the password itself. Arguably it is moot rather than open, since there is nothing left to guess, but it is left Active and unanswered rather than quietly closed: if authentication ever returns, this threat returns with it, and it should be visible when that happens.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: A-5
  ROLE: Threatens

[THREAT]
UID: TH-09
STATEMENT: >>>
**Denial of service**: socket exhaustion, oversized bodies, slow clients.
<<<
VIA: >>>
Boundaries `B-2` and `B-4`; see section 3 of [`security.md`](security.md).
<<<
CONSEQUENCE: >>>
Loss of the web interface. Crucially **not** loss of control: the safety supervisor is a separate task on the other core ([LINK: SWA-15]), and [LINK: SWR-NFR-02] bounds non-safety interference at 50 ms.
<<<
SAFETY_RELEVANT: No
STATUS: Active
COMMENT: >>>
Deliberately carries no `Threatens` relation. The interface is not one of the assets, and the asset an attacker would want, [LINK: A-1], is exactly the one this threat cannot reach.
<<<

[THREAT]
UID: TH-10
STATEMENT: >>>
**Information disclosure through unauthenticated reads.**
<<<
VIA: >>>
Every `GET` is unauthenticated by design.
<<<
CONSEQUENCE: >>>
Telemetry, logs, programs, run history, network status ([LINK: A-7]).
<<<
SAFETY_RELEVANT: No
STATUS: Active
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: A-7
  ROLE: Threatens
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ADV-1
  ROLE: Exploited by

[THREAT]
UID: TH-11
STATEMENT: >>>
**Open provisioning access point.**
<<<
VIA: >>>
[LINK: SWR-NET-02] AP fallback; if `net.ap_pass` is empty the AP is open.
<<<
CONSEQUENCE: >>>
Anyone in radio range reaches the provisioning page and can supply their own credentials.
<<<
SAFETY_RELEVANT: Yes
STATUS: Active
COMMENT: >>>
**Has no security goal.** The stale appendix routed it through `SEC-01`, which is withdrawn. This is the most security-sensitive surface the device has and the one most likely to be reachable without credentials, and it is currently answered by nothing but [LINK: OQ-S4]. Carried as [LINK: SRR-08].
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: A-4
  ROLE: Threatens
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HZ-12
  ROLE: Reaches

[THREAT]
UID: TH-12
STATEMENT: >>>
**Physical console access.**
<<<
VIA: >>>
UART console.
<<<
CONSEQUENCE: >>>
The simulator build exposes fault injection and direct commands.
<<<
SAFETY_RELEVANT: Yes
STATUS: Active
COMMENT: >>>
On a simulator build only: the console is compile-gated behind `CONFIG_KILN_PLANT_SIM` and is not in a production image. [LINK: SWR-NFR-24] is the requirement behind that.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: A-1
  ROLE: Threatens
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ADV-3
  ROLE: Exploited by
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HZ-12
  ROLE: Reaches

[THREAT]
UID: TH-13
STATEMENT: >>>
**mDNS and network reconnaissance.**
<<<
VIA: >>>
`kiln.local` advertised on the LAN ([LINK: SWR-NET-05]).
<<<
CONSEQUENCE: >>>
Makes the device trivially discoverable; a precondition for the rest rather than a threat alone.
<<<
SAFETY_RELEVANT: No
STATUS: Active
COMMENT: >>>
**Has no security goal, and never had one.** Discoverability is accepted: the device is meant to be found by its operator, and `SWR-NET-05` asks for exactly that. Recorded so the acceptance is explicit rather than an omission.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ADV-1
  ROLE: Exploited by

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: SECURITY-4
TITLE: 4. Security goals

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
`IMPLEMENTATION` is what is actually true today, and the marks are not
decoration: most of this is **not built yet**.

- **Built**, implemented and covered by an automated test.
- **Partial**, implemented in the host-testable core, but the piece that would
  enforce it on a device does not exist.
- **Spec**, required or designed, no implementation at all.
- **Absent**, not required, not designed, not present. A gap, named as one.
- **NotNeeded**, the threat it would answer cannot occur.

At the time of writing there is **no HTTP transport on the target**. The REST API
is complete and host-tested, but the `esp_http_server` adapter that would
terminate TCP, parse headers, check a password and apply rate limiting is not
started. Nearly every network control here is therefore `Spec`, and that is the
single most important fact in this document.
<<<

[SECURITY_GOAL]
UID: SEC-00
STATEMENT: >>>
**No network request shall change any device state at all.**
<<<
IMPLEMENTATION: Built
STATUS: Active
COMMENT: >>>
Tested: every request other than `GET` returns `403 read_only`, program authoring included since 2026-10-06, and the handlers are **deleted from the image** rather than gated by a flag. This is the strongest control in this document, and the only one that does not depend on the unwritten HTTP transport, because it is enforced in the API layer that *is* written.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: TH-01
  ROLE: Addresses
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: TH-02
  ROLE: Addresses
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: TH-03
  ROLE: Addresses
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: TH-04
  ROLE: Addresses
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: TH-05
  ROLE: Addresses
- TYPE: Child
  VALUE: SWR-WEB-26
  ROLE: Realised by

[SECURITY_GOAL]
UID: SEC-01
STATEMENT: >>>
Only an authenticated client may change state.
<<<
IMPLEMENTATION: Partial
STATUS: Withdrawn
COMMENT: >>>
**Dissolved 2026-10-06**: no client may change state, authenticated or not, so there is nothing left for authentication to gate. Kept rather than deleted because the route-selection half is still in the code and still worth not losing: `kiln_api_needs_auth` returns true for every non-`GET` method, so a route added later is protected *by default* rather than by somebody remembering to list it. Superseded in substance by [LINK: SEC-00].
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: TH-01
  ROLE: Addresses
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: TH-02
  ROLE: Addresses
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: TH-03
  ROLE: Addresses

[SECURITY_GOAL]
UID: SEC-02
STATEMENT: >>>
Credentials are never disclosed through the API.
<<<
IMPLEMENTATION: Built
STATUS: Active
COMMENT: >>>
Tested: `net.wifi_pass`, `net.ap_pass` and `security.web_password` carry `KILN_CFG_F_SECRET`, and the config endpoint returns only whether a secret *is set*, never its value. There is no mode in which it returns one, and the endpoint is generated from the same table that declares the flag, so a new secret field is redacted by construction rather than by a second edit.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: TH-06
  ROLE: Addresses
- TYPE: Child
  VALUE: SWR-CFG-07
  ROLE: Realised by

[SECURITY_GOAL]
UID: SEC-03
STATEMENT: >>>
Every byte from the network is treated as hostile.
<<<
IMPLEMENTATION: Built
STATUS: Active
COMMENT: >>>
A declared maximum body per handler (2048 B), a bounded token budget (192), a bounded point budget (2000), caller-owned buffers, no allocation proportional to input, and responses streamed in bounded chunks rather than accumulated. Exercised by a fuzz suite and by ASan/UBSan in CI. The firmware contains zero `malloc`/`free`, so an attacker cannot exhaust a heap that does not exist.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: TH-09
  ROLE: Addresses
- TYPE: Child
  VALUE: SWR-NFR-19
  ROLE: Realised by

[SECURITY_GOAL]
UID: SEC-04
STATEMENT: >>>
Authentication resists guessing and timing analysis.
<<<
IMPLEMENTATION: Spec
STATUS: Withdrawn
COMMENT: >>>
**Dissolved 2026-10-06** with the password itself: there is nothing left to authenticate. This also closed [LINK: SRR-02], which asked whether the password was stored as a salted hash or in plaintext. It leaves [LINK: TH-08] with no goal, which is recorded there rather than hidden here.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: TH-08
  ROLE: Addresses

[SECURITY_GOAL]
UID: SEC-05
STATEMENT: >>>
Only a valid image for this target can be installed, and never mid-firing.
<<<
IMPLEMENTATION: Spec
STATUS: Active
COMMENT: >>>
Note what is *not* required anywhere: a **signature**. See [LINK: SRR-04]. With [LINK: TH-04] closed by removing the network update path entirely, this goal currently has no path to exercise, which is why it remains `Spec` rather than becoming moot: whatever replaces OTA under [LINK: OQ-08] will need it.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: TH-04
  ROLE: Addresses
- TYPE: Child
  VALUE: SWR-UPD-02
  ROLE: Realised by
- TYPE: Child
  VALUE: SWR-UPD-03
  ROLE: Realised by
- TYPE: Child
  VALUE: SWR-UPD-04
  ROLE: Realised by
- TYPE: Child
  VALUE: SWR-UPD-08
  ROLE: Realised by

[SECURITY_GOAL]
UID: SEC-06
STATEMENT: >>>
The device reveals and transmits nothing to third parties.
<<<
IMPLEMENTATION: Built
STATUS: Active
COMMENT: >>>
Structurally: there is no cloud client, no telemetry, no analytics and no outbound connection in the codebase. This is a property of what was never written, which is why it carries no threat relation: it answers no specific threat and forecloses a category.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Child
  VALUE: SWR-NFR-21
  ROLE: Realised by
- TYPE: Child
  VALUE: UR-CON-03
  ROLE: Realised by

[SECURITY_GOAL]
UID: SEC-07
STATEMENT: >>>
No hidden access.
<<<
IMPLEMENTATION: Built
STATUS: Active
COMMENT: >>>
No hardcoded credentials, no undocumented ports, no default-enabled remote access. Built by inspection; [LINK: SWA-16] reinforces it, because the UI uses only the public API, so there is no privileged back channel to find.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: TH-10
  ROLE: Addresses
- TYPE: Child
  VALUE: SWR-NFR-22
  ROLE: Realised by

[SECURITY_GOAL]
UID: SEC-08
STATEMENT: >>>
A compromised or overloaded web stack cannot affect control.
<<<
IMPLEMENTATION: Built
STATUS: Active
COMMENT: >>>
[LINK: SWA-15] pins control and safety to core 1 and networking to core 0; [LINK: SWA-13] uses queues and immutable snapshots with no mutex on the control path; [LINK: SWR-NFR-02] bounds interference at 50 ms; [LINK: SWA-04] keeps heat authority in the safety supervisor alone. This is the strongest security property the design has, and it came from the safety work.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: TH-09
  ROLE: Addresses
- TYPE: Child
  VALUE: SWR-NFR-02
  ROLE: Realised by
- TYPE: Child
  VALUE: SWA-13
  ROLE: Realised by
- TYPE: Child
  VALUE: SWA-15
  ROLE: Realised by
- TYPE: Child
  VALUE: SWA-04
  ROLE: Realised by

[SECURITY_GOAL]
UID: SEC-09
STATEMENT: >>>
Secrets at rest are protected from physical access.
<<<
IMPLEMENTATION: Absent
STATUS: Active
COMMENT: >>>
No flash encryption, no secure boot, neither appears in `sdkconfig.defaults`. Carried as [LINK: SRR-05], and open as [LINK: OQ-S3].
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: TH-06
  ROLE: Addresses
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: TH-12
  ROLE: Addresses

[SECURITY_GOAL]
UID: SEC-10
STATEMENT: >>>
Browser-originated requests cannot be forged.
<<<
IMPLEMENTATION: NotNeeded
STATUS: Active
COMMENT: >>>
CSRF matters because a forged request can act; with the interface read-only a forged request can only read, and the reads are public anyway ([LINK: SRR-07]). This is the one gap the read-only decision closed without any code. It is `NotNeeded` rather than `Absent` on that basis, and it reverts to `Absent` the moment any write route returns: [LINK: SRR-06] keeps that visible.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: TH-05
  ROLE: Addresses

[SECURITY_GOAL]
UID: SEC-11
STATEMENT: >>>
Traffic is confidential and integrity-protected in transit.
<<<
IMPLEMENTATION: Absent
STATUS: Active
COMMENT: >>>
Absent **by decision, not oversight**: see [LINK: SRR-03] for the trade. [LINK: SWR-NFR-20]'s "trusted LAN only" is what stands in its place.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: TH-07
  ROLE: Addresses
- TYPE: Child
  VALUE: SWR-NFR-20
  ROLE: Realised by

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: SECURITY-5
TITLE: 5. Security residual risk

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
What stands after the goals above, stated without softening. Each names who
carries it. There is no `SRR-10`: the numbering has a gap and keeping it is
cheaper than renumbering IDs that are cited from other documents.
<<<

[SECURITY_RESIDUAL_RISK]
UID: SRR-01
STATEMENT: >>>
**There is no authentication on a device, because there is no HTTP server on a device.** Every network control is a specification.
<<<
WHY_IT_STANDS: >>>
Milestone M6 is not implemented.
<<<
CARRIED_BY: >>>
The project.
<<<
MITIGATION: >>>
None. It is not a *live* exposure, since an unimplemented server accepts no connections, but it must not be read as "the controls exist". **Nothing in this document may be cited as evidence of a deployed control.**
<<<
STATUS: Active
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SEC-05
  ROLE: Remains under

[SECURITY_RESIDUAL_RISK]
UID: SRR-02
STATEMENT: >>>
The design and the data model disagree about the password.
<<<
WHY_IT_STANDS: >>>
It does not. **Closed 2026-10-06**: `security.web_password` is removed from the configuration schema. There is no password, no hash, and nothing to disagree about.
<<<
CARRIED_BY: >>>
Nobody.
<<<
STATUS: Resolved
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SEC-04
  ROLE: Remains under

[SECURITY_RESIDUAL_RISK]
UID: SRR-03
STATEMENT: >>>
**No TLS.** Every request crosses the LAN in cleartext, readable by [LINK: ADV-1].
<<<
WHY_IT_STANDS: >>>
A self-signed certificate on a `.local` name trains users to click through browser warnings, costs flash and RAM against a 2 MB OTA slot, and still cannot be verified. The honest trade was plaintext on a network already assumed trusted.
<<<
CARRIED_BY: >>>
The owner of the network ([LINK: SYS-ASM-05]).
<<<
MITIGATION: >>>
[LINK: SWR-NFR-20]'s "trusted LAN only", stated in the README, in [`safety.md`](safety.md) and here. It is a real limitation, not a solved problem.
<<<
STATUS: Active
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SEC-11
  ROLE: Remains under

[SECURITY_RESIDUAL_RISK]
UID: SRR-04
STATEMENT: >>>
**Firmware images are validated but not authenticated.** [LINK: SWR-UPD-03] requires checking an image is *valid for this target*; no requirement anywhere asks for a **signature**, and secure boot is not enabled.
<<<
WHY_IT_STANDS: >>>
Signing was never specified.
<<<
CARRIED_BY: >>>
The project.
<<<
MITIGATION: >>>
Only [LINK: SWR-UPD-08]'s authentication stands between an attacker and [LINK: TH-04], and that is specification only. With secure boot absent, physical reflash ([LINK: ADV-3]) bypasses it entirely. Open as [LINK: OQ-S2].
<<<
STATUS: Active
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SEC-05
  ROLE: Remains under

[SECURITY_RESIDUAL_RISK]
UID: SRR-05
STATEMENT: >>>
**Secrets are plaintext in flash.** No flash encryption. Anyone with the board reads the user's WiFi password.
<<<
WHY_IT_STANDS: >>>
Not specified; flash encryption also complicates development and field recovery.
<<<
CARRIED_BY: >>>
The owner, and anyone who disposes of a device.
<<<
MITIGATION: >>>
None. [LINK: ADV-4] is the under-considered case: a device sold on or thrown out carries the credentials with it. Open as [LINK: OQ-S3].
<<<
STATUS: Active
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SEC-09
  ROLE: Remains under

[SECURITY_RESIDUAL_RISK]
UID: SRR-06
STATEMENT: >>>
**No CSRF defence.** A page the operator visits can issue cross-origin requests to `kiln.local`, and DNS rebinding defeats the same-origin policy.
<<<
WHY_IT_STANDS: >>>
Not specified or designed. Currently harmless only because the interface is read-only, which is a property of [LINK: SEC-00] rather than of any CSRF work.
<<<
CARRIED_BY: >>>
The project.
<<<
MITIGATION: >>>
None. Cheap to fix, since validating `Origin` and `Host` costs almost nothing, and it should land *with* the transport rather than after it.
<<<
STATUS: Active
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SEC-10
  ROLE: Remains under

[SECURITY_RESIDUAL_RISK]
UID: SRR-07
STATEMENT: >>>
**All reads are unauthenticated**, and reads are now all there is.
<<<
WHY_IT_STANDS: >>>
The interface is read-only by decision.
<<<
CARRIED_BY: >>>
The design, deliberately.
<<<
MITIGATION: >>>
Defensible: the dashboard is meant to be glanceable and the data is low-value ([LINK: A-7]). But it is a decision, and it means the device is not private. Open as [LINK: OQ-S5].
<<<
STATUS: Active
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SEC-07
  ROLE: Remains under

[SECURITY_RESIDUAL_RISK]
UID: SRR-08
STATEMENT: >>>
**The AP fallback may be open.** [LINK: SWR-NET-02] starts an access point with a provisioning page; nothing requires `net.ap_pass` to be non-empty.
<<<
WHY_IT_STANDS: >>>
No minimum-passphrase rule exists.
<<<
CARRIED_BY: >>>
The installer.
<<<
MITIGATION: >>>
None. The provisioning page is the most security-sensitive surface the device has, and it is the one most likely to be reachable without credentials. Open as [LINK: OQ-S4].
<<<
STATUS: Active
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: TH-11
  ROLE: Remains against

[SECURITY_RESIDUAL_RISK]
UID: SRR-09
STATEMENT: >>>
**[LINK: SYS-ASM-05] is the assumption everything rests on, and it is usually false.** Home and studio LANs routinely carry compromised IoT devices and guest traffic.
<<<
WHY_IT_STANDS: >>>
A single-purpose controller cannot police its own network.
<<<
CARRIED_BY: >>>
The owner.
<<<
MITIGATION: >>>
A VLAN or a dedicated network segment is the real answer; the documentation should say so rather than only saying "trusted".
<<<
STATUS: Active
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SEC-11
  ROLE: Remains under

[SECURITY_RESIDUAL_RISK]
UID: SRR-11
STATEMENT: >>>
**No field update path.** Removing OTA closed [LINK: TH-04] completely and left the device with no way to receive a fix, including a security fix, without physical access.
<<<
WHY_IT_STANDS: >>>
[LINK: SWR-UPD-01] was inverted deliberately; the replacement is [LINK: OQ-08] and is not yet specified.
<<<
CARRIED_BY: >>>
The project. **This blocks release**, not merely an open question.
<<<
MITIGATION: >>>
None. A vulnerability found after shipping currently requires visiting every device.
<<<
STATUS: Active
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: TH-04
  ROLE: Remains against

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: SECURITY-6
TITLE: 6. Open questions

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Each names the residual risk it would settle, so a question cannot drift away
from the risk that motivated it. Two were answered by the read-only decision of
2026-10-06 and are kept as `Resolved` rather than deleted, because the answer is
the useful part.
<<<

[OPEN_QUESTION]
UID: OQ-S1
STATEMENT: >>>
Salted hash or plaintext for the web password?
<<<
STATUS: Resolved
COMMENT: >>>
**Answered 2026-10-06 by removing the question.** The interface is read-only, so there is no password. Closed [LINK: SRR-02] with it.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SRR-02
  ROLE: Would settle

[OPEN_QUESTION]
UID: OQ-S2
STATEMENT: >>>
Should firmware images be **signed** ([LINK: SWR-UPD-03] requires validity, not authenticity), and should secure boot be enabled?
<<<
STATUS: Active
COMMENT: >>>
Both have real field-recovery costs for an open-source device users are expected to build themselves. Bound up with [LINK: OQ-08], since whatever replaces the removed update path has to answer this at the same time.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SRR-04
  ROLE: Would settle

[OPEN_QUESTION]
UID: OQ-S3
STATEMENT: >>>
Should flash encryption be enabled by default?
<<<
STATUS: Active
COMMENT: >>>
It complicates self-builders and field debugging, and it is the only answer to [LINK: SRR-05].
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SRR-05
  ROLE: Would settle

[OPEN_QUESTION]
UID: OQ-S4
STATEMENT: >>>
Should `net.ap_pass` have an enforced minimum, or should the access point refuse to start open?
<<<
STATUS: Active
COMMENT: >>>
[LINK: TH-11] currently has no security goal at all, so this question is the only thing standing against it.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SRR-08
  ROLE: Would settle

[OPEN_QUESTION]
UID: OQ-S5
STATEMENT: >>>
Should read endpoints require authentication when a password is set?
<<<
STATUS: Resolved
COMMENT: >>>
**Moot 2026-10-06**: there is no password to set. The question of whether the dashboard should be private *at all* is not moot and remains as [LINK: SRR-07].
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SRR-07
  ROLE: Would settle

[[/SECTION]]
